<h1>Exercise Name: Seated Single-Arm Dumbbell Wrist Curl (Palms-Up Dumbbell Wrist Curl)</h1>
    <u><b><h4>Exercise Overview</h4></b></u>
This is an isolation exercisedesigned to target the inner part of the forearm.

Target Muscles:Forearm Flexors (Flexor Carpi Radialis, Flexor Carpi Ulnaris, and Palms-Up Wrist Flexors)—highlighted in orange/redin the animation.
Secondary Muscles:Grip strength muscles in the hand and fingers.

</div></div>
<h1>How to Perform (Step-by-Step)</h1><u><b><h4>1. Starting Position</h4></b></u>
:Sit comfortably on a flat bench.
Pick up a dumbbell in one hand with an underhand grip(palm facing up).
Rest your forearm flat along your thigh (or on the bench), letting your wrist and hand hang just over the edge of your knee.


<u><b><h4>2. The Execution (Curl Up)</h4></b></u>
:Exhale and slowly curl your wrist upward as high as possible without lifting your forearm off your thigh.
Pause briefly at the top to squeeze the forearm muscle.


<u><b><h4>3. The Lowering Phase</h4></b></u>
:Inhale and slowly lower the dumbbell back down to the starting position.
(Optional advanced touch): Allow the dumbbell to roll slightly down to your fingertips at the very bottom for a deeper stretch, then curl back up.




 <h1> Key Benefits</h1>

<b>◉Forearm Muscle Hypertrophy:</b> Directly builds thick, defined, and aesthetic inner forearms.<br>

<b>◉Improved Grip Strength:</b> Helps you hold heavier weights in exercises like deadlifts, pull-ups, and rows.<br>

<b>◉Enhanced Wrist Stability:</b> Strengthens the wrist joints, reducing the risk of strains during heavy upper-body pressing or pulling lifts.<br>

<b>◉Athletic Performance:</b> Great for sports that require strong wrist control (e.g., tennis, rock climbing, martial arts, cricket).


<h1> Recommended Sets & Repetitions</h1>

Forearm flexors respond best to moderate-to-high repetition rangesand controlled time-under-tension:

| Level | Sets | Repetitions | Rest Time |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Beginner| 3 Sets | 12 – 15 Reps | 45–60 sec |
| Intermediate / Advanced| 3 – 4 Sets | 15 – 20 Reps | 45–60 sec |

> Pro Tip:Keep the weight moderate and focus on slow, controlled movement (2 seconds up, 2–3 seconds down). Avoid swinging or lifting your arm off your knee.


